aw me and those who  did not know me sought and desired the  acquaintance for my reputation the  hardships that i underwent in this were  long to tell as well as peril and  privation as of storms and cold  often times they overtook me alone and  in the wilderness but i came forth from  them all by the great mercy of god our  lord  i was in this country nearly six years  alone among the indians and naked  like them  i moved on and remained with the  avavares people eight months reckoned by  the number of moons in all this time  people came to see us from many parts  and they said that most truly we were  children of the sun because of the great  solicitation made by these coming from  different parts to find us  we all became physicians although in  being venturous and bold to attempt the  performance of any cure i was the most  remarkable no one whom we treated but  told us that he left well and so great  was the confidence that they would  become healed if we administered to them  they even believed that whilst we  remained none of them could die  these and the rest of the people behind  related an extraordinary circumstance  and by the way they counted there  appeared to be 15 or 16 years since it  occurred  they said that a man wandered through  the country whom they called bad thing  he was small of body and wore a beard  and they never distinctly saw his  features  when he came to the house where they  lived their hair stood up and trembled  when he entered and seized whom he chose  and giving them three great gashes in  the side with a very sharp flint he put  his hand through them drawing forth the  entrails from one of which he would cut  off a portion more or less the length of  a palm and throw it on the embers  a little after this he would begin to  unite it and putting his hands on the  wounds these would instantly become  healed  these things they told us of we much  laughed at and ridiculed and they seeing  our incredulity brought to us many of  those they said he had seized  we told them he was an evil one and in  the best way we could gave them to  understand that if they would believe in  god our lord and become christians like  us they need have no fear of him nor  would he dare to come and inflict these  injuries and they might be certain he  would not venture to appear while we  remained in the land  they were all ignorant of time either by  the sun or moon nor do they reckon by  the month or year they better know and  understand the differences of the  seasons when the fruits come to ripen  where the fish resort and the position  of the stars which they are ready and  practiced  we always went naked like them and  covered ourselves at night with  deerskins  the children are suckled until the age  of 12 years when they are old enough to  get support for themselves  we asked why they rear them in this  manner and they said because of the  great poverty of the land  they are all war-like and have as much  strategy for protecting themselves  against enemies as they could have where  they reared in italy in continual feuds  they are the most watchful in danger of  any people i know if they fear an enemy  they are awake all night long even with  a bow at his side and a dozen arrows  they all differ in their habitations  towns and tongues everywhere they  produce stupefaction with a smoke  and for that they will give whatever  they possess  when the women have their indisposition  they seek food only for themselves as no  one else will eat of what they bring  in the time i was among these people i  witnessed a diabolical practice  a man living with another one of those  who are emasculate and impotent  these go habited like women and perform  their duties use the bow and carry heavy  loads among them we saw many mutilated  in the way i describe  they are more muscular than the other  men and taller  they bear very weighty burdens  when we saw sure signs of christians and  heard how near we were to them we gave  thanks to lord our god for having chosen  to bring us out of a captivity so  melancholy and wretched the delight we  felt let each one conjecture when he  shall remember the length of time we  were in that country the suffering and  perils we underwent we traveled 25  leagues little more or less and reached  a town of friendly indians the alcalde  left us there and went on three leagues  further to a town called kuliakan where  was melchior diaz principal alcalde and  the captain of the province the alcalde  mayor knew of the expedition and hearing  of our return he immediately left that  night and came to where we were  he wept with us giving praise to god our  lord for having extended over us such  great care  he comforted and entertained us  hospitably we left for mexico  many came out on the roads to gaze at us  giving thanks to god for having saved us  from so many calamities  we arrived at mexico on sunday the day  before the vespers of santiago where we  were handsomely treated by the viceroy  and the marquis de valle and welcomed  with joy they gave us clothing and  offered whatever they had  on the day of santiago was a celebration  and a joust of reeds with bulls  you
 

